<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vilnius</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wilno, Vilna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vilnius is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, where the Vilnia flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neris">Neris</a>. Made the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a> in the early fourteenth century, it grew into a centre of Catholic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Jewish life, with a university from 1579, and was called the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> of Lithuania'. Contested with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after 1918, it returned to Lithuania in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vilnius.jpg" alt="Vilnius" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BigHead · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vilnius_old_town_by_Augustas_Didzgalvis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vilnius lies at the confluence of the Vilnia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neris">Neris</a> rivers in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, 312 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and the main Lithuanian seaport, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a>. The city covers 402 km2, of which 68.8 per cent is green space. Its name comes from the river Vilnia, an old Lithuanian name meaning ripple, which was transferred to the city. The name first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> letters of Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a> in 1323, in the form Vilna, and in 1325 as Wilno, a form still used in Polish. The Lithuanian form Vilnius was recorded around 1600 in the Postil of Mikalojus Daukša and became widespread during the national revival at the end of the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history of Vilnius goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>. From at least 1323 until 1795 the city was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and from the 13th century until the mid-17th century the Vilnius Castle Complex was the residence of the Lithuanian monarchs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights were granted in 1378, and a magistrate or city council, subordinate to the ruler, then governed it.</p><p>In 1517 the Italian princess Bona Sforza married the Lithuanian Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a>, which strengthened the Italian influence on the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architecture. Vilnius became an artistic centre of the Grand Duchy, attracting artists from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> it was noted for its multicultural population, which contemporary sources compared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> it was one of Europe's most important Jewish centres.</p><p>Later Vilnius was ruled by imperial and Soviet Russia, Napoleonic France, Imperial and Nazi Germany and interwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and it again became a capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in the 20th century. Before World War II Poles made up the majority of inhabitants, and the population exchange between Poland and Soviet Lithuania after the war changed that.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vilnius is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the most populous city in the Baltic states. Its Old Town, considered one of the largest and best-preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1994. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style known as Vilnian Baroque is named after the city, which is the easternmost Baroque city and the largest north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Around two thirds of its residents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vilnius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Pereiaslav a joint Cossack–Muscovite army invaded Lithuania in 1654, took White Ruthenia and <b>Vilnius</b>, and Khmelnytsky halted a Polish–Tatar counterstroke at the Battle of Okhmativ in 1655.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Gediminas made <b>Vilnius</b> his capital, and in letters of 1323 he invited merchants, craftsmen and clergy from the West to settle in his realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A conference in <b>Vilnius</b> in September 1917 elected a twenty-member Council of Lithuania, the Taryba, chaired by Antanas Smetona, and on 16 February 1918 its members, among them Jonas Basanavičius, signed the Act of Independence, proclaiming an independent democratic state with Vilnius as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The German occupation government permitted a <b>Vilnius</b> Conference in September 1917, which elected a 20-member Council of Lithuania (Taryba).</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Vilna</b> Ghetto in Lithuania the song's text was the basis of a Yiddish song: one account credits Shmerke Kaczerginski's &quot;Partizaner-Marsh&quot; of 1943, which refers to the Jewish resistance group FPO (Fareynikte Partizaner Organizatsye), another names a Yiddish version by Leyb Rozental.</blockquote><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a></li><li><blockquote>Elijah of <b>Vilna</b> (1720–1789) warned singers not to pause in the middle of one phrase, since a pause changed its sense.</blockquote><a href="/songs/shalom-aleichem">Shalom Aleichem</a></li></ul></section>
